<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Itagaki Taisuke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Itagaki Taisuke was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the Tosa domain who commanded troops for the new imperial government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> of 1868–1869 and served in the early Meiji government. Leaving it in 1873 over a proposed expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, he led the Freedom and People's Rights Movement for an elected assembly and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s first national political party, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a>, in 1881.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/itagaki-taisuke.jpg" alt="Itagaki Taisuke" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Itagaki_Taisuke_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Itagaki was born on 21 May 1837 into a middle-ranking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> family in Tosa Domain, in present-day Kōchi Prefecture, and became a Councillor of State (sangi) in 1869. In 1873 the first major schism of the Meiji oligarchy came over the Seikanron, the proposed military expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Itagaki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a> argued that it would give an outlet to the disaffected samurai. Leaders just back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iwakura-mission">Iwakura Mission</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi">Ōkubo Toshimichi</a>, held that internal reform came first, and the proposal was rejected in October 1873. Itagaki resigned. Unlike Saigō, who later led an armed rebellion, he chose political means: &quot;Saigō fights the government with arms, but we will fight it with people's rights.&quot;</p><p>On 12 January 1874 he formed the Aikoku Kōtō, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s first political association, and on 17 January he and eight associates submitted a memorial calling for an elected assembly. Ōkubo dismissed it, but its publication in the press set off a nationwide debate. In April 1874 Itagaki founded the Risshisha in Tosa. He rejoined the government after the Osaka Conference of 1875, persuaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> and Inoue Kaoru, but resigned in October 1875.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>After the Imperial Rescript of 1881 promised a national assembly by 1890, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> (Jiyūtō) was founded on 29 October 1881 with Itagaki as president. Its prospectus, written mainly by Nakae Chōmin, declared liberty the natural state of man, and its support came chiefly from rural landowners resentful of the land tax. On 6 April 1882 an elementary school teacher stabbed Itagaki at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu">Gifu</a>; he is reported to have cried, &quot;Itagaki may die, but liberty will not!&quot;</p><p>From November 1882 to June 1883 he toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with Gotō Shōjirō and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-spencer">Herbert Spencer</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The tour, funded by the Mitsui company, brought accusations that he had been bought off. Peasant uprisings such as the Chichibu incident of 1884 divided the party, which dissolved on 29 October 1884. His own early view of the franchise was elitist, for the 1874 memorial would have limited it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> and wealthier farmers and merchants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The party was refounded in 1890. In June 1898 it merged with Ōkuma Shigenobu's Progressive Party, and on 30 June 1898 the Ōkuma–Itagaki cabinet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s first party-based government, was formed, with Itagaki as Minister of Home Affairs. It fell within four months, and Itagaki resigned on 27 October 1898. He retired in 1900 and died on 16 July 1919. Some contemporaries called him an opportunist, but his choice of agitation over armed revolt set a precedent for non-violent opposition in modern Japan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Itagaki Taisuke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It became Kochi Prefecture in 1871, and <b>Itagaki Taisuke</b> later led the democratic movement from Tosa.</blockquote><a href="/state/tosa-domain">Tosa Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kōchi-shi, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Shibakōen, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.56,133.53&amp;year=1890" aria-label="Kōchi-shi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Itagaki Taisuke"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5955 4639h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5955 4639h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.56,133.53&amp;year=1890">What was here in 1890? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
